const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Regulatory Writing Service","serviceType":"Regulatory, clinical, safety and medical device documentation for global submissions","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/medical-writing/regulatory-writing/","description":"Clinical, safety, device and lifecycle regulatory documents written to the instrument that governs each — ICH E6(R3), M4(R4), E2C(R2), E2F.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What types of regulatory documents do you write?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Clinical and nonclinical: protocols to ICH M11 and E6(R3), investigator’s brochures, informed consent documents, clinical study reports to E3, nonclinical summaries, patient narratives and case listings. Dossier: CTD Modules 2 to 5 to M4(R4), with Module 1 handled per region. Applications and correspondence: INDs, NDAs, BLAs, MAAs, IMPDs, briefing books, meeting packages, responses to agency questions, rebuttal letters. Safety: PBRERs to E2C(R2), DSURs to E2F, risk management plans to EU GVP Module V, safety specifications and pharmacovigilance plans to E2E, expedited reports to E2A, post-approval case handling to E2D(R1). Devices and IVDs: clinical evaluation reports under MDR Annex XIV, performance evaluation reports under IVDR Annex XIII, technical documentation, clinical investigation plans to ISO 14155:2026, instructions for use and labelling to ISO 20417:2026, post-market surveillance and PMCF. Software: clinical evaluation to MDCG 2020-1 and lifecycle documentation to IEC 62304. Lifecycle: SmPCs, PILs, variations, renewals. And lay summaries under CTR Article 37(4)."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is your typical turnaround?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Short regulatory summaries and single documents: 7 to 10 business days. Full clinical study reports: 15 to 25 business days. Complex dossiers and multi-module CTDs: 25 or more business days. These bands have not changed since we first published them, because they were accurate when we set them. Two things affect them more than document length does. First, how complete the source material is: a CSR waiting on final statistical outputs is not a writing problem. Second, your review cycles, which are almost always the long pole — a document drafted in fifteen days and reviewed over six weeks took eight weeks. Expedited delivery is available where capacity allows and is quoted on capacity rather than on urgency; where a date is not achievable we will tell you rather than take the fee and miss it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which ICH guideline governs a PSUR?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"ICH E2C(R2), which defines the Periodic Benefit-Risk Evaluation Report — the PBRER, which is what most people still mean when they say PSUR. E2C(R2) reached Step 4 on 15 November 2012 and extended the report’s scope from safety to benefit-risk. DSURs are governed by ICH E2F, Step 4 on 17 August 2010. E2E is Pharmacovigilance Planning — it covers the safety specification and the pharmacovigilance plan, principally in preparation for the early post-marketing period, and it governs no periodic report at all. We answer this in detail because it is commonly got wrong, and the error is easy to miss: “compliance with GVP and ICH E2E guidelines” gets attached to packages containing PSURs and DSURs. It is a small citation error and it is exactly the kind that tells a pharmacovigilance reader whether a vendor knows the territory."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is ICH-GCP still E6(R2)?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. ICH E6(R3) is current: the Principles and Annex 1 were adopted by ICH and by the CHMP on 23 July 2025 and are described as a replacement for E6(R2), and the FDA issued its final E6(R3) guidance in September 2025. Annex 2, which covers non-traditional interventional designs including pragmatic and decentralised trials, was adopted in June 2026 and applies in the EU from 15 January 2027; a consolidated guideline covering all three parts was published in July 2026. A protocol or SOP written in 2026 that still cites E6(R2) as the current standard is a year out of date. The substantive change is proportionality: R3 asks for quality management built around the risks that actually matter to participant safety and to the reliability of the results, rather than uniform procedure applied regardless of risk — which is generally good news for investigator-initiated and lower-risk studies."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I still cite MEDDEV 2.7/1 rev 4 in a clinical evaluation?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Carefully, and not as the governing framework. MEDDEV 2.7/1 rev 4 was issued in 2016 under the Directives, before the MDR applied. It has not been formally withdrawn, and industry does still use it — but it is not listed as current MDR guidance, and MDCG 2020-6 Appendix I identifies the sections of it that remain relevant under the MDR. That is the accurate position and the defensible way to cite it: structure the clinical evaluation to MDR Annex XIV with the MDCG series applied, and reference MEDDEV rev 4 where MDCG 2020-6 says it still holds. The MDCG documents that matter most for a CER are 2020-6 on sufficient clinical evidence for legacy devices, 2020-5 on equivalence, 2020-13 on the assessment report template, and 2020-1 where the device contains software — which is the one most often missing from device CERs that happen to include software."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need to move to eCTD v4.0?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not yet, anywhere — it is mandatory in no region today. The FDA has accepted v4.0 for new applications since 16 September 2024, forward compatibility for existing v3.2.2 applications is not yet available, and no date has been announced on which v4.0 becomes the only accepted version. The EMA is the only agency with a published timetable: optional for new centrally authorised MAAs since 22 December 2025, strongly recommended from Q1 2027, and mandatory for new CAP MAAs from Q1 2028. Health Canada has not implemented it and its Canadian Module 1 guide for v4 remains in draft. The MHRA and TGA continue in v3.2.2. On Japan: PMDA accepts v4.0 and its Japanese implementation guide is at v1.6.0 dated 10 March 2025, but PMDA has published no mandatory transition deadline — a claim of a 2026 Japanese mandate circulates widely in consultancy material and we could not verify it from PMDA’s own publications, so we do not repeat it."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do you handle confidentiality and conflicts?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A non-disclosure agreement is signed before any material moves, and writers and project staff are bound by internal confidentiality agreements as well. Unpublished clinical data, submission drafts and agency correspondence are held under the UK GDPR on access-controlled systems within a quality management system certified to ISO 9001:2015 — certificate H3CR9TCU20251212INDQM10N — kept only as long as the project needs them, never used to train anything, and never shared outside your project team. On conflicts, which matter more in this category than in most: we do not take competing products in the same indication concurrently, and where a potential conflict exists we tell you before accepting the work rather than managing it quietly. If you require a writer to work only within your own document management system rather than ours, we do that; several of our clients require it and it is a reasonable thing to require."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I see a sample of your regulatory writing before ordering?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and there is a full one on this page — a document set QC report for a Class IIb device submission, with the device, the manufacturer and every figure changed. Open it above and read the whole thing without asking us for anything. If you want a sample closer to your own work, ask and we will build one around a document type like yours: same product class, same submission route, same governing instruments. Two honest caveats. Client work is confidential, so any sample is redacted or composite — a vendor who sends you somebody else’s actual CER has told you something important about how they will treat yours. And a sample shows structure, citation discipline and QC method; it cannot show whether the writer understood your product, which is what the free scoping conversation is for."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will you speed up our approval?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Suppliers offer to “accelerate your approval timelines”. Approval timelines belong to the agency: review clocks are set by regulation and by the agency’s own procedures, and no writer affects them. What a writing partner genuinely affects is the submission-preparation timeline, and the number of questions you receive afterwards. A submission whose documents agree with each other, whose claims are all evidenced, and whose standards are all current generates questions about the science rather than about the paperwork — and a deficiency letter is the thing that actually costs months. That is a real benefit and it is worth stating accurately rather than as an outcome we do not control."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What happens if I am not satisfied with the document?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"We revise it until it meets the brief and the instrument it is written to, with no cap on rounds inside the scope agreed. Two things make that workable rather than open-ended. First, the structure is agreed against the governing guideline before any prose is written, so a revision is a revision rather than a restart — most dissatisfaction with a regulatory document traces back to a structure nobody signed off. Second, scope is written down: a different indication, a second region’s Module 1, or a document type not in the brief is new work, and we say which side of that line something falls on before starting rather than after. Where the problem is ours — a wrong version cited, a claim untraced, a number inconsistent across documents — correcting it is free and you hear about it from us first."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Regulatory writing","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/medical-writing/regulatory-writing/"}]}];

